1
00:00:04,319 --> 00:00:10,080
Mr. Pocket said he was glad to see me, and he hoped I was not sorry to see him. “For,

2
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:17,798
I really am not,” he added, with his son’s smile, “an alarming personage.” He was a young-looking

3
00:00:17,798 --> 00:00:24,719
man, in spite of his perplexities and his very grey hair, and his manner seemed quite natural.

4
00:00:24,719 --> 00:00:30,638
I use the word natural, in the sense of its being unaffected; there was something comic in

5
00:00:30,638 --> 00:00:36,719
his distraught way, as though it would have been downright ludicrous but for his own perception

6
00:00:36,719 --> 00:00:43,278
that it was very near being so. When he had talked with me a little, he said to Mrs. Pocket, with a

7
00:00:43,278 --> 00:00:49,477
rather anxious contraction of his eyebrows, which were black and handsome, “Belinda, I hope you

8
00:00:49,478 --> 00:00:56,520
have welcomed Mr. Pip?” And she looked up from her book, and said, “Yes.” She then smiled upon me in

9
00:00:56,520 --> 00:01:02,599
an absent state of mind, and asked me if I liked the taste of orange-flower water? As the question

10
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:08,759
had no bearing, near or remote, on any foregone or subsequent transaction, I consider it to have been

11
00:01:08,759 --> 00:01:14,840
thrown out, like her previous approaches, in general conversational condescension.

12
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:20,638
I found out within a few hours, and may mention at once, that Mrs. Pocket was the only daughter of a

13
00:01:20,638 --> 00:01:25,719
certain quite accidental deceased Knight, who had invented for himself a conviction that his

14
00:01:25,719 --> 00:01:31,159
deceased father would have been made a Baronet but for somebody’s determined opposition arising out

15
00:01:31,159 --> 00:01:37,159
of entirely personal motives,—I forget whose, if I ever knew,—the Sovereign’s, the Prime Minister’s,

16
00:01:37,159 --> 00:01:42,359
the Lord Chancellor’s, the Archbishop of Canterbury’s, anybody’s,—and had tacked himself

17
00:01:42,359 --> 00:01:48,319
on to the nobles of the earth in right of this quite supposititious fact. I believe he had been

18
00:01:48,319 --> 00:01:53,118
knighted himself for storming the English grammar at the point of the pen, in a desperate address

19
00:01:53,118 --> 00:01:58,240
engrossed on vellum, on the occasion of the laying of the first stone of some building or other,

20
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:03,519
and for handing some Royal Personage either the trowel or the mortar. Be that as it may,

21
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:08,478
he had directed Mrs. Pocket to be brought up from her cradle as one who in the nature of things must

22
00:02:08,478 --> 00:02:14,240
marry a title, and who was to be guarded from the acquisition of plebeian domestic knowledge.

23
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:18,798
So successful a watch and ward had been established over the young lady by this judicious

24
00:02:18,798 --> 00:02:25,840
parent, that she had grown up highly ornamental, but perfectly helpless and useless. With her

25
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:31,879
character thus happily formed, in the first bloom of her youth she had encountered Mr. Pocket:

26
00:02:31,879 --> 00:02:37,319
who was also in the first bloom of youth, and not quite decided whether to mount to the Woolsack,

27
00:02:37,319 --> 00:02:43,798
or to roof himself in with a mitre. As his doing the one or the other was a mere question of time,

28
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:48,798
he and Mrs. Pocket had taken Time by the forelock (when, to judge from its length, it would seem to

29
00:02:48,798 --> 00:02:54,840
have wanted cutting), and had married without the knowledge of the judicious parent. The judicious

30
00:02:54,840 --> 00:03:00,680
parent, having nothing to bestow or withhold but his blessing, had handsomely settled that dower

31
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:06,919
upon them after a short struggle, and had informed Mr. Pocket that his wife was “a treasure for a

32
00:03:06,919 --> 00:03:13,438
Prince.” Mr. Pocket had invested the Prince’s treasure in the ways of the world ever since,

33
00:03:13,438 --> 00:03:19,278
and it was supposed to have brought him in but indifferent interest. Still, Mrs. Pocket was in

34
00:03:19,278 --> 00:03:24,958
general the object of a queer sort of respectful pity, because she had not married a title;

35
00:03:24,958 --> 00:03:31,319
while Mr. Pocket was the object of a queer sort of forgiving reproach, because he had never got one.

36
00:03:31,319 --> 00:03:35,637
Mr. Pocket took me into the house and showed me my room: which was a pleasant one,

37
00:03:36,278 --> 00:03:42,000
and so furnished as that I could use it with comfort for my own private sitting-room. He then

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,478
knocked at the doors of two other similar rooms, and introduced me to their occupants, by name

39
00:03:48,478 --> 00:03:55,159
Drummle and Startop. Drummle, an old-looking young man of a heavy order of architecture,

40
00:03:55,159 --> 00:04:01,879
was whistling. Startop, younger in years and appearance, was reading and holding his head,

41
00:04:01,879 --> 00:04:07,478
as if he thought himself in danger of exploding it with too strong a charge of knowledge.

42
00:04:07,478 --> 00:04:13,319
Both Mr. and Mrs. Pocket had such a noticeable air of being in somebody else’s hands, that I

43
00:04:13,319 --> 00:04:17,477
wondered who really was in possession of the house and let them live there,

44
00:04:17,478 --> 00:04:23,039
until I found this unknown power to be the servants. It was a smooth way of going on,

45
00:04:23,039 --> 00:04:28,559
perhaps, in respect of saving trouble; but it had the appearance of being expensive, for the

46
00:04:28,559 --> 00:04:33,278
servants felt it a duty they owed to themselves to be nice in their eating and drinking, and to

47
00:04:33,278 --> 00:04:39,798
keep a deal of company downstairs. They allowed a very liberal table to Mr. and Mrs. Pocket,

48
00:04:39,798 --> 00:04:44,359
yet it always appeared to me that by far the best part of the house to have boarded in would

49
00:04:44,359 --> 00:04:50,159
have been the kitchen,—always supposing the boarder capable of self-defence, for, before I

50
00:04:50,159 --> 00:04:55,118
had been there a week, a neighbouring lady with whom the family were personally unacquainted,

51
00:04:55,118 --> 00:05:01,958
wrote in to say that she had seen Millers slapping the baby. This greatly distressed Mrs. Pocket,

52
00:05:01,958 --> 00:05:06,958
who burst into tears on receiving the note, and said that it was an extraordinary thing that the

53
00:05:06,958 --> 00:05:13,879
neighbours couldn’t mind their own business. By degrees I learnt, and chiefly from Herbert,

54
00:05:13,879 --> 00:05:20,118
that Mr. Pocket had been educated at Harrow and at Cambridge, where he had distinguished himself;

55
00:05:20,118 --> 00:05:24,798
but that when he had had the happiness of marrying Mrs. Pocket very early in life,

56
00:05:24,798 --> 00:05:31,199
he had impaired his prospects and taken up the calling of a Grinder. After grinding a number of

57
00:05:31,199 --> 00:05:37,840
dull blades,—of whom it was remarkable that their fathers, when influential, were always going to

58
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:44,520
help him to preferment, but always forgot to do it when the blades had left the Grindstone,—he had

59
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:51,359
wearied of that poor work and had come to London. Here, after gradually failing in loftier hopes,

60
00:05:51,359 --> 00:05:56,240
he had “read” with divers who had lacked opportunities or neglected them, and had

61
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:01,398
refurbished divers others for special occasions, and had turned his acquirements to the account

62
00:06:01,398 --> 00:06:07,398
of literary compilation and correction, and on such means, added to some very moderate private

63
00:06:07,398 --> 00:06:14,477
resources, still maintained the house I saw. Mr. and Mrs. Pocket had a toady neighbour;

64
00:06:14,478 --> 00:06:20,719
a widow lady of that highly sympathetic nature that she agreed with everybody, blessed everybody,

65
00:06:20,719 --> 00:06:27,398
and shed smiles and tears on everybody, according to circumstances. This lady’s name was Mrs.

66
00:06:27,398 --> 00:06:34,039
Coiler, and I had the honour of taking her down to dinner on the day of my installation. She gave me

67
00:06:34,039 --> 00:06:40,519
to understand on the stairs, that it was a blow to dear Mrs. Pocket that dear Mr. Pocket should

68
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:47,000
be under the necessity of receiving gentlemen to read with him. That did not extend to me,

69
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,159
she told me in a gush of love and confidence (at that time, I had known her something

70
00:06:52,159 --> 00:06:58,958
less than five minutes); if they were all like Me, it would be quite another thing.

71
00:06:58,958 --> 00:07:04,559
“But dear Mrs. Pocket,” said Mrs. Coiler, “after her early disappointment (not that

72
00:07:04,559 --> 00:07:09,599
dear Mr. Pocket was to blame in that), requires so much luxury and elegance—”

73
00:07:09,599 --> 00:07:14,359
“Yes, ma’am,” I said, to stop her, for I was afraid she was going to cry.

74
00:07:14,359 --> 00:07:17,958
“And she is of so aristocratic a disposition—”

75
00:07:17,958 --> 00:07:22,599
“Yes, ma’am,” I said again, with the same object as before.

76
00:07:22,599 --> 00:07:25,118
“—That it is hard,” said Mrs. Coiler,

77
00:07:25,118 --> 00:07:30,680
“to have dear Mr. Pocket’s time and attention diverted from dear Mrs. Pocket.”

78
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:34,118
I could not help thinking that it might be harder if the butcher’s

79
00:07:34,118 --> 00:07:39,039
time and attention were diverted from dear Mrs. Pocket; but I said nothing,

80
00:07:39,039 --> 00:07:44,039
and indeed had enough to do in keeping a bashful watch upon my company manners.

81
00:07:44,039 --> 00:07:49,118
It came to my knowledge, through what passed between Mrs. Pocket and Drummle while I was

82
00:07:49,118 --> 00:07:54,159
attentive to my knife and fork, spoon, glasses, and other instruments of self-destruction,

83
00:07:54,159 --> 00:08:02,000
that Drummle, whose Christian name was Bentley, was actually the next heir but one to a baronetcy.

84
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:08,240
It further appeared that the book I had seen Mrs. Pocket reading in the garden was all about titles,

85
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:12,840
and that she knew the exact date at which her grandpapa would have come into the book,

86
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:19,478
if he ever had come at all. Drummle didn’t say much, but in his limited way (he struck

87
00:08:19,478 --> 00:08:24,599
me as a sulky kind of fellow) he spoke as one of the elect, and recognised Mrs. Pocket as a

88
00:08:24,599 --> 00:08:29,919
woman and a sister. No one but themselves and Mrs. Coiler the toady neighbour showed

89
00:08:29,919 --> 00:08:35,319
any interest in this part of the conversation, and it appeared to me that it was painful to Herbert;

90
00:08:36,278 --> 00:08:41,477
but it promised to last a long time, when the page came in with the announcement of a domestic

91
00:08:41,478 --> 00:08:48,638
affliction. It was, in effect, that the cook had mislaid the beef. To my unutterable amazement,

92
00:08:48,638 --> 00:08:54,319
I now, for the first time, saw Mr. Pocket relieve his mind by going through a performance

93
00:08:54,319 --> 00:08:59,558
that struck me as very extraordinary, but which made no impression on anybody else,

94
00:08:59,559 --> 00:09:05,359
and with which I soon became as familiar as the rest. He laid down the carving-knife

95
00:09:05,359 --> 00:09:11,758
and fork,—being engaged in carving, at the moment,—put his two hands into his disturbed hair,

96
00:09:11,759 --> 00:09:17,240
and appeared to make an extraordinary effort to lift himself up by it. When he had done this,

97
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:23,759
and had not lifted himself up at all, he quietly went on with what he was about.

98
00:09:23,759 --> 00:09:29,680
Mrs. Coiler then changed the subject and began to flatter me. I liked it for a few moments,

99
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:34,759
but she flattered me so very grossly that the pleasure was soon over. She had a serpentine

100
00:09:34,759 --> 00:09:39,319
way of coming close at me when she pretended to be vitally interested in the friends and

101
00:09:39,319 --> 00:09:45,438
localities I had left, which was altogether snaky and fork-tongued; and when she made an occasional

102
00:09:45,438 --> 00:09:52,159
bounce upon Startop (who said very little to her), or upon Drummle (who said less), I rather envied

103
00:09:52,159 --> 00:09:58,319
them for being on the opposite side of the table. After dinner the children were introduced, and

104
00:09:58,319 --> 00:10:04,798
Mrs. Coiler made admiring comments on their eyes, noses, and legs,—a sagacious way of improving

105
00:10:04,798 --> 00:10:11,000
their minds. There were four little girls, and two little boys, besides the baby who might

106
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,438
have been either, and the baby’s next successor who was as yet neither. They were brought in by

107
00:10:17,438 --> 00:10:22,159
Flopson and Millers, much as though those two non-commissioned officers had been recruiting

108
00:10:22,159 --> 00:10:27,080
somewhere for children and had enlisted these, while Mrs. Pocket looked at the young Nobles that

109
00:10:27,080 --> 00:10:31,519
ought to have been as if she rather thought she had had the pleasure of inspecting them before,

110
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:36,680
but didn’t quite know what to make of them. “Here! Give me your fork, Mum, and take

111
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:42,438
the baby,” said Flopson. “Don’t take it that way, or you’ll get its head under the table.”

112
00:10:42,438 --> 00:10:46,118
Thus advised, Mrs. Pocket took it the other way, and got its head

113
00:10:46,118 --> 00:10:51,638
upon the table; which was announced to all present by a prodigious concussion.

114
00:10:51,638 --> 00:10:59,039
“Dear, dear! Give it me back, Mum,” said Flopson; “and Miss Jane, come and dance to baby, do!”

115
00:10:59,039 --> 00:11:04,759
One of the little girls, a mere mite who seemed to have prematurely taken upon herself some charge of

116
00:11:04,759 --> 00:11:11,520
the others, stepped out of her place by me, and danced to and from the baby until it left off

117
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:17,798
crying, and laughed. Then, all the children laughed, and Mr. Pocket (who in the meantime

118
00:11:17,798 --> 00:11:24,559
had twice endeavoured to lift himself up by the hair) laughed, and we all laughed and were glad.

119
00:11:24,559 --> 00:11:29,159
Flopson, by dint of doubling the baby at the joints like a Dutch doll,

120
00:11:29,159 --> 00:11:34,398
then got it safely into Mrs. Pocket’s lap, and gave it the nut-crackers to play with;

121
00:11:34,398 --> 00:11:39,359
at the same time recommending Mrs. Pocket to take notice that the handles of that instrument were

122
00:11:39,359 --> 00:11:46,039
not likely to agree with its eyes, and sharply charging Miss Jane to look after the same. Then,

123
00:11:46,039 --> 00:11:51,319
the two nurses left the room, and had a lively scuffle on the staircase with a dissipated page

124
00:11:51,319 --> 00:11:56,679
who had waited at dinner, and who had clearly lost half his buttons at the gaming-table.

125
00:11:56,680 --> 00:12:00,879
I was made very uneasy in my mind by Mrs. Pocket’s falling into a discussion with

126
00:12:00,879 --> 00:12:06,919
Drummle respecting two baronetcies, while she ate a sliced orange steeped in sugar and wine,

127
00:12:06,919 --> 00:12:10,919
and, forgetting all about the baby on her lap, who did most appalling things

128
00:12:10,919 --> 00:12:17,958
with the nut-crackers. At length little Jane, perceiving its young brains to be imperilled,

129
00:12:17,958 --> 00:12:24,919
softly left her place, and with many small artifices coaxed the dangerous weapon away.

130
00:12:24,919 --> 00:12:31,520
Mrs. Pocket finishing her orange at about the same time, and not approving of this, said to Jane,—

131
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:37,199
“You naughty child, how dare you? Go and sit down this instant!”

132
00:12:37,199 --> 00:12:43,558
“Mamma dear,” lisped the little girl, “baby ood have put hith eyeth out.”

133
00:12:43,559 --> 00:12:50,438
“How dare you tell me so?” retorted Mrs. Pocket. “Go and sit down in your chair this moment!”

134
00:12:50,438 --> 00:12:52,679
Mrs. Pocket’s dignity was so crushing,

135
00:12:52,680 --> 00:12:58,319
that I felt quite abashed, as if I myself had done something to rouse it.

136
00:12:58,319 --> 00:13:02,637
“Belinda,” remonstrated Mr. Pocket, from the other end of the table,

137
00:13:02,638 --> 00:13:08,240
“how can you be so unreasonable? Jane only interfered for the protection of baby.”

138
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:13,919
“I will not allow anybody to interfere,” said Mrs. Pocket. “I am surprised, Matthew,

139
00:13:13,919 --> 00:13:17,438
that you should expose me to the affront of interference.”

140
00:13:17,438 --> 00:13:22,519
“Good God!” cried Mr. Pocket, in an outbreak of desolate desperation. “Are

141
00:13:22,520 --> 00:13:27,719
infants to be nut-crackered into their tombs, and is nobody to save them?”

142
00:13:27,719 --> 00:13:33,039
“I will not be interfered with by Jane,” said Mrs. Pocket, with a majestic glance at that

143
00:13:33,039 --> 00:13:40,039
innocent little offender. “I hope I know my poor grandpapa’s position. Jane, indeed!”

144
00:13:40,039 --> 00:13:45,000
Mr. Pocket got his hands in his hair again, and this time really did lift himself some inches out

145
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:51,879
of his chair. “Hear this!” he helplessly exclaimed to the elements. “Babies are to be nut-crackered

146
00:13:51,879 --> 00:13:59,798
dead, for people’s poor grandpapa’s positions!” Then he let himself down again, and became silent.

147
00:13:59,798 --> 00:14:05,240
We all looked awkwardly at the tablecloth while this was going on. A pause succeeded,

148
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:10,798
during which the honest and irrepressible baby made a series of leaps and crows at little Jane,

149
00:14:10,798 --> 00:14:13,080
who appeared to me to be the only member of the

150
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:18,879
family (irrespective of servants) with whom it had any decided acquaintance.

151
00:14:18,879 --> 00:14:23,719
“Mr. Drummle,” said Mrs. Pocket, “will you ring for Flopson? Jane,

152
00:14:23,719 --> 00:14:29,359
you undutiful little thing, go and lie down. Now, baby darling, come with ma!”

153
00:14:29,359 --> 00:14:34,519
The baby was the soul of honour, and protested with all its might. It doubled itself up the

154
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:40,438
wrong way over Mrs. Pocket’s arm, exhibited a pair of knitted shoes and dimpled ankles to the company

155
00:14:40,438 --> 00:14:46,278
in lieu of its soft face, and was carried out in the highest state of mutiny. And it gained its

156
00:14:46,278 --> 00:14:53,598
point after all, for I saw it through the window within a few minutes, being nursed by little Jane.

157
00:14:53,599 --> 00:14:57,919
It happened that the other five children were left behind at the dinner-table,

158
00:14:57,919 --> 00:15:02,478
through Flopson’s having some private engagement, and their not being anybody

159
00:15:02,478 --> 00:15:09,599
else’s business. I thus became aware of the mutual relations between them and Mr. Pocket,

160
00:15:09,599 --> 00:15:14,798
which were exemplified in the following manner. Mr. Pocket, with the normal

161
00:15:14,798 --> 00:15:20,438
perplexity of his face heightened and his hair rumpled, looked at them for some minutes,

162
00:15:20,438 --> 00:15:25,080
as if he couldn’t make out how they came to be boarding and lodging in that establishment,

163
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:31,759
and why they hadn’t been billeted by Nature on somebody else. Then, in a distant Missionary way

164
00:15:31,759 --> 00:15:38,958
he asked them certain questions,—as why little Joe had that hole in his frill, who said, Pa, Flopson

165
00:15:38,958 --> 00:15:44,719
was going to mend it when she had time,—and how little Fanny came by that whitlow, who said, Pa,

166
00:15:44,719 --> 00:15:51,240
Millers was going to poultice it when she didn’t forget. Then, he melted into parental tenderness,

167
00:15:51,240 --> 00:15:56,679
and gave them a shilling apiece and told them to go and play; and then as they went out,

168
00:15:56,680 --> 00:16:03,240
with one very strong effort to lift himself up by the hair he dismissed the hopeless subject.

169
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:08,679
In the evening there was rowing on the river. As Drummle and Startop had each a boat,

170
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:14,958
I resolved to set up mine, and to cut them both out. I was pretty good at most exercises in which

171
00:16:14,958 --> 00:16:21,199
country boys are adepts, but as I was conscious of wanting elegance of style for the Thames,—not

172
00:16:21,199 --> 00:16:27,039
to say for other waters,—I at once engaged to place myself under the tuition of the winner of

173
00:16:27,039 --> 00:16:33,798
a prize-wherry who plied at our stairs, and to whom I was introduced by my new allies.

174
00:16:33,798 --> 00:16:39,159
This practical authority confused me very much by saying I had the arm of a blacksmith. If he

175
00:16:39,159 --> 00:16:45,199
could have known how nearly the compliment lost him his pupil, I doubt if he would have paid it.

176
00:16:45,199 --> 00:16:48,759
There was a supper-tray after we got home at night, and I think we should all have enjoyed

177
00:16:48,759 --> 00:16:54,798
ourselves, but for a rather disagreeable domestic occurrence. Mr. Pocket was in good spirits,

178
00:16:54,798 --> 00:17:00,080
when a housemaid came in, and said, “If you please, sir, I should wish to speak to you.”

179
00:17:00,080 --> 00:17:05,118
“Speak to your master?” said Mrs. Pocket, whose dignity was roused again.

180
00:17:05,118 --> 00:17:11,840
“How can you think of such a thing? Go and speak to Flopson. Or speak to me—at some other time.”

181
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:15,359
“Begging your pardon, ma’am,” returned the housemaid,

182
00:17:15,359 --> 00:17:19,758
“I should wish to speak at once, and to speak to master.”

183
00:17:19,759 --> 00:17:26,359
Hereupon, Mr. Pocket went out of the room, and we made the best of ourselves until he came back.

184
00:17:26,359 --> 00:17:31,000
“This is a pretty thing, Belinda!” said Mr. Pocket, returning with a countenance

185
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:37,759
expressive of grief and despair. “Here’s the cook lying insensibly drunk on the kitchen floor,

186
00:17:37,759 --> 00:17:42,879
with a large bundle of fresh butter made up in the cupboard ready to sell for grease!”

187
00:17:42,879 --> 00:17:50,478
Mrs. Pocket instantly showed much amiable emotion, and said, “This is that odious Sophia’s doing!”

188
00:17:50,478 --> 00:17:54,559
“What do you mean, Belinda?” demanded Mr. Pocket.

189
00:17:54,559 --> 00:17:59,879
“Sophia has told you,” said Mrs. Pocket. “Did I not see her with my own eyes and

190
00:17:59,879 --> 00:18:04,118
hear her with my own ears, come into the room just now and ask to speak to you?”

191
00:18:04,118 --> 00:18:08,000
“But has she not taken me downstairs, Belinda,” returned Mr. Pocket,

192
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,319
“and shown me the woman, and the bundle too?”

193
00:18:11,319 --> 00:18:16,398
“And do you defend her, Matthew,” said Mrs. Pocket, “for making mischief?”

194
00:18:16,398 --> 00:18:19,319
Mr. Pocket uttered a dismal groan.

195
00:18:19,319 --> 00:18:26,840
“Am I, grandpapa’s granddaughter, to be nothing in the house?” said Mrs. Pocket. “Besides,

196
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:31,879
the cook has always been a very nice respectful woman, and said in the most natural manner

197
00:18:31,879 --> 00:18:38,118
when she came to look after the situation, that she felt I was born to be a Duchess.”

198
00:18:38,118 --> 00:18:43,278
There was a sofa where Mr. Pocket stood, and he dropped upon it in the attitude of the Dying

199
00:18:43,278 --> 00:18:50,118
Gladiator. Still in that attitude he said, with a hollow voice, “Good night, Mr. Pip,” when I deemed

200
00:18:50,118 --> 00:18:52,991
it advisable to go to bed and leave him. Chapter 24.
